The Security Council,

Recalling its resolution 1970 (2011) and all subsequent relevant resolutions, including resolutions 2292 (2016), 2357 (2017), 2420 (2018), 2473 (2019), 2526 (2020), 2578 (2021), 2635 (2022), 2684 (2023), 2733 (2024), 2780 (2025) and 2796 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of Libya,

Reaffirming its resolution 2796 (2025), and stressing the importance of a Libyan-led and Libyan-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, including the full implementation of the road map announced by the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on 21 August 2025, and stressing that the United Nations Support Mission in Libya should focus on its core political mission in line with the strategic review and establish clear performance benchmarks,

Expressing grave concern at the continued fragile political and security situation in Libya, including armed clashes and the activities of militias and armed groups, and their impact on the stability of Libya and the region,

Reaffirming the obligation of all parties in Libya to fully respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and stressing the need to protect civilians, including migrants and refugees, and to ensure accountability for violations and abuses,

Expressing grave concern at the recent loss of life in the Mediterranean Sea off the coast of Libya, underscoring the need for comprehensive approaches to migration and refugee protection in accordance with international law, including by addressing root causes, saving lives and ensuring full respect for the human rights of migrants and refugees, and stressing the importance of countering migrant smuggling and human trafficking, including through enhanced regional cooperation and support for Libyan coast guard capacity-building in accordance with international law, and holding perpetrators accountable,

Reiterating its concern that the illicit proliferation of arms and related materiel in Libya continues to undermine peace, security and stability, and emphasizing the importance of full and effective implementation of the arms embargo by all Member States and all Libyan parties, bearing in mind the views of the Libyan authorities and the need to keep the authorizations under review in light of their effectiveness and actual needs,

Recalling the authorizations put in place by resolution 2292 (2016), extended and amended by subsequent relevant resolutions, for the strict implementation of the arms embargo on the high seas off the coast of Libya,

Welcoming the continued contribution of the European Union Naval Force Mediterranean Operation IRINI to the implementation of the arms embargo, in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions,

Taking note of the reports of the Secretary-General on the implementation of the relevant resolutions and emphasizing the importance of continued and timely reporting,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend the authorizations as set out in resolution 2733 (2024), as extended by resolution 2780 (2025), for a further six months from the date of this resolution, and stresses that these authorizations shall be implemented in full accordance with international law, including the law of the sea;

2. Calls upon all Member States and all Libyan parties to fully and effectively implement the arms embargo, including by preventing the illicit transfer of arms and related materiel to and from Libya through all relevant means, including by air, land and sea, in full compliance with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea and relevant human rights and refugee law, with due regard for the sovereignty and jurisdiction of States, and without impeding legitimate trade, humanitarian deliveries, or the export of Libyan oil and gas; to refrain from any actions, including external interference, that could undermine the political process, peace and stability of Libya; to cooperate fully with the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011) and the Panel of Experts, including through timely reporting on inspections, seizures and violations and by sharing relevant information; and calls for enhanced coordination between relevant international actors, including Operation IRINI, and the Government of National Unity of Libya and relevant Libyan institutions, with full respect for Libya's sovereignty, territorial integrity and national unity;

3. Calls for the complete and verifiable withdrawal of all foreign fighters and mercenaries from Libya without further delay, in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions;

4. Calls upon all Member States implementing the authorizations in paragraph 1 to ensure that seized arms and related materiel are handled and disposed of transparently and in accordance with international law, including through timely notification and consultation with the Libyan authorities; decides that the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1970 (2011) shall play a leading role in the disposal of prohibited arms and related materiel seized pursuant to the authorizations in paragraph 1; requests Member States to notify the Committee of any inspection, seizure or disposal without delay; and calls for capacity-building and training of the relevant Libyan departments to enable them to implement the arms embargo independently in the future;

5. Calls upon all Member States to ensure that implementation of the measures authorized in this resolution does not impede humanitarian assistance or search and rescue operations at sea, and fully respects the human rights of all persons;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within five months of the adoption of this resolution on its implementation, including a detailed assessment of the implementation of the inspections regime, including the number and outcome of inspections, any detected arms embargo violations, and the impact on legitimate maritime activity and humanitarian deliveries; an assessment of the effectiveness of Operation IRINI; information on the handling, disposal, destruction or transfer of seized contraband; information on cooperation with the United Nations Support Mission in Libya and Operation IRINI; information on the humanitarian and migration situation; and recommendations for improving the effectiveness of the measures, including any necessary adjustment; and calls for greater transparency and accountability by all Member States and relevant operations;

7. Decides to review the implementation of this resolution after three months on the basis of an oral update by the Secretary-General on the effectiveness and transparency of the inspections regime, without affecting the duration of the authorizations set out in paragraph 1;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

